// mgeHost64 — H2b: HEIGHT-FIELD OCCLUSION for the WATER MIRROR, shared by cull.comp AND
// cullscatter.comp. SHADER-ONLY header, exactly like hizocclusion.h.fsl: cull.srt.h stays the
// C++-safe one; include this AFTER it (needs gCullParams, gSkyHeightMin, gHeightOccParams).
//
// ⚠ A SHARED HEADER IS WHAT MAKES THE AGREEMENT STRUCTURAL. cull.comp sizes the prefix-sum runs and
// cullscatter.comp fills them, and they re-run the identical per-instance test to do it. If the two
// disagree by even one instance the scatter writes into runs the sum did not size — which shows up
// as a gpuCull MATCH failure, not as corruption, and only if someone is looking. Putting the test in
// one place makes "they agree" a property of the code rather than a rule to remember, which is the
// argument cellown.h.fsl and hizocclusion.h.fsl both already make.
//
// ── WHAT THIS TESTS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The main view has a depth buffer of itself and therefore a Hi-Z (hizocclusion.h.fsl). The mirror
// does not, and it draws ~86% instances that cannot be seen (H0's bracket, [85.9%, 87.0%], robust to
// a 9x coarser field). What it has instead is a HEIGHT FIELD of the world — pSkyHeight, rebuilt on
// snap crossings, statics rasterised in with no distance limit — and H1's MIN-pyramid over it.
//
// So: march the field along the ray from the mirrored eye to the instance's bound, and reject if the
// field stands above the ray anywhere along it. This is occprobe.comp.fsl's mirror half, promoted
// from a measurement to a cull, with one substitution: the probe's brute-force (2R+1)² gather
// becomes one load of the pyramid, which is the same quantity precomputed (verified: H1's acceptance
// table).
//
// ⚠ THE MARCH STARTS AT THE WATER-PLANE CROSSING, NOT AT THE MIRRORED EYE. The mirror camera sits
// BELOW the water plane whenever the real one is above it, so a march started there begins
// underground and reports everything blocked. The physical path is camera -> water surface ->
// object, and only the segment past the surface is in the air where an above-water occluder stands.
//
// ⚠ AND IT STOPS SHORT: `effR + texel + margin`. The instance is ITSELF in the field, so the last
// texels of the march hold its own roof, and without stopping short every object reports itself
// occluded by itself.
//
// ── WHY EVERY UNCERTAIN CASE RETURNS NOT-OCCLUDED ────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A false negative draws something hidden: a few wasted pixels in a mip-chained, refracted
// reflection. A false positive deletes a building from the water. Off the map window, no valid
// pyramid, target under the surface, degenerate ray — all pass. That asymmetry is also why the tap
// count is a cost knob and not a correctness one: under-sampling under-rejects.

// True when the instance's bound sphere is hidden from the MIRRORED eye by the height field.
// c_rel = the instance's bound centre RELATIVE to gCullParams.eye (which for this lane is the
// mirror's own eye, published by dlReflectGeoCull); effR its radius.
bool heightOccluded(float3 c_rel, float effR)
{
    float4 hp = gHeightOccParams.arm;     // x = armed, y = water plane Z (absolute), z = steps, w = bias
    if (hp.x < 0.5f)
        return false;                     // this lane does not run the test (every lane but the mirror)

    float4 mp = gHeightOccParams.map;     // xy = map origin (absolute corner), z = u/texel, w = resolution
    float texel = max(mp.z, 1.0f);
    float res   = max(mp.w, 1.0f);
    int   level = (int)gHeightOccParams.arm2.x;   // pyramid level the taps read
    float margin = gHeightOccParams.arm2.y;
    // The tap footprint in WORLD units at the level actually being read. Level N texels are
    // 2^N times wider than level 0, and the end margin below is measured in that width — a margin
    // that used the level-0 texel would let the last tap land in a block partly filled by the
    // instance's own roof. (The min reduction mostly protects against that on its own: a block that
    // is half roof and half open ground reduces to the ground and does not block. Mostly is not a
    // guarantee, and widening the margin errs toward NOT occluding, which is the safe direction.)
    float texelL = texel * exp2((float)level);

    // Back to ABSOLUTE world, which is the space the height field is indexed in. c_rel is relative
    // to the mirror's eye, so the eye itself is the only thing that has to be added back.
    float3 eyeAbs = float3(gCullParams.eye.x, gCullParams.eye.y, gCullParams.eye.z);
    float  waterZ = hp.y;

    // The MIRRORED eye: the reflection's rays leave from here. Below the plane whenever the camera
    // is above it, which is precisely why the march cannot start at it.
    float3 eyeM = float3(eyeAbs.x, eyeAbs.y, 2.0f * waterZ - eyeAbs.z);
    if (eyeM.z >= waterZ)
        return false;                     // camera at or under the surface — not this ray

    // The TOP of the bound sphere, and after trying the centre this is the right choice for TWO
    // separate reasons — one geometric, one about which instances survive to be tested at all.
    //
    // GEOMETRY. The rays that hit the sphere sweep a cone about the CENTRE ray whose cross-section
    // at fraction u is a disc of radius effR*u. Over any horizontal point of that disc, the HIGHEST
    // such ray is the one over the axis, at centre-ray-z + effR*u — and that is exactly the TOP ray,
    // since the top ray sits effR*u above the centre ray at every u. So "min-field over the disc >
    // top-ray z" is precisely the hidden condition. Aiming at the top and ALSO adding effR*u would
    // double-count and demand the field clear centre + 2*effR*u, twice what geometry asks.
    //
    // POPULATION, and this is what the centre-aimed experiment actually taught. `t0` — where the
    // sightline leaves the water — is computed on this ray, and t0 >= 1 bails the instance. Aim at
    // the CENTRE and every static whose bound centre sits at or below the water plane bails, which
    // on a coast or a river is a large slice of the set: measured 53.5% rejected against the top
    // ray's 69.6%, i.e. aiming lower LOST rejections by disqualifying the instances rather than by
    // failing the test. The top is the part of the object that is above water and visible, so it is
    // also the honest thing to ask the water-crossing question about.
    float3 tgt = float3(eyeAbs.x + c_rel.x, eyeAbs.y + c_rel.y, eyeAbs.z + c_rel.z + effR);

    float denom = tgt.z - eyeM.z;
    if (denom <= 1.0e-3f)
        return false;                     // target at/below the mirrored eye — no above-water segment
    float t0 = (waterZ - eyeM.z) / denom;
    if (t0 >= 1.0f)
        return false;                     // wholly under water; the reflect clip plane owns it
    t0 = max(t0, 0.0f);
    float3 S = eyeM + (tgt - eyeM) * t0;  // where the sightline leaves the water

    float2 dxy = tgt.xy - S.xy;
    float  L   = length(dxy);
    // ⚠ 2*effR, NOT effR — the cone test made this margin load-bearing in a new way. The last tap's
    // footprint now reaches coneR (~effR at the far end) BEYOND the sample point toward the target,
    // so a march stopping only effR short would put the object's OWN roof inside its own final tap
    // and every large static would report itself occluded by itself. Clearing the object's radius
    // twice — once for the tap's reach, once for the object — is what keeps that impossible.
    float  end = 2.0f * effR + texelL + margin;
    if (L <= end)
        return false;                     // too close for the field to separate the object from its
                                          // own footprint
    float tEnd = (L - end) / L;

    // UNIFORM spacing, unlike sunocc.comp's geometric march, and the difference is the ray's shape.
    // The sun march is semi-infinite from the surface it shades, so its near taps decide almost
    // everything. This segment is BOUNDED at both ends — water surface to object — and a blocking
    // ridge is as likely at its middle as at either end, so there is no place to concentrate samples.
    int   steps = (int)max(hp.z, 1.0f);
    float bias  = hp.w;
    float maxLevel = max(mp.w > 1.0f ? floor(log2(mp.w)) : 1.0f, 1.0f);   // log2(res) = top mip

    // ══ THE OCCLUDEE IS A SPHERE, SO THE TEST IS A CONE — NOT A RAY ═════════════════════════════
    //
    // ⚠⚠ THIS IS THE FIX FOR REAL FALSE POSITIVES: geometry that IS visible in the mirror was being
    // culled, which reads on screen as holes in the reflection showing the bright sky/fog behind.
    //
    // The march used to test ONE ray, to the TOP of the bound sphere, and H0's probe said in its own
    // comment why that is only an upper bound: "rays to the sphere's left and right edges leave on
    // DIFFERENT BEARINGS". Vertically the single ray is sound — every ray from this origin to a lower
    // point of the sphere lies under it, so a field that blocks the top ray blocks those. Horizontally
    // it is not: a ridge can block the centre bearing while the object is plainly visible around one
    // side of it. For a MEASUREMENT that slack is harmless (it inflates a ceiling). Promoted into a
    // CULL it deletes buildings from the water.
    //
    // The honest test is the CONE the sphere subtends from the mirrored eye. At a point a fraction u
    // of the way along eyeM->target, the cone's cross-section radius is effR*u — zero at the eye,
    // effR at the object. The sphere is hidden only if the field covers that whole disc, which is two
    // requirements and this codebase already has the machinery for both:
    //
    //   VERTICAL   — already satisfied by aiming at the sphere's TOP: that ray is the highest of the
    //                bundle over every point of the disc, so clearing it clears all of them. This is
    //                why there is no `+ coneR` in the compare.
    //   HORIZONTAL — THE ACTUAL FIX. The min must be taken over a footprint at least as wide as the
    //                cone, not over one texel on the axis. That is exactly what a coarser PYRAMID
    //                LEVEL is: level L's texel spans texel0 * 2^L and holds the min over all of it.
    //                So pick L from the cone diameter and read four corner taps there.
    //
    // Four corner taps of the cone's bounding box at that level, min'd — hizocclusion.h.fsl's own
    // argument, for the same reason: one tap at the centre does not cover a disc that straddles a
    // texel boundary, four at the bbox corners do once the texel is at least the disc's diameter.
    //
    // ⚠ THE COST OF BEING RIGHT IS YIELD, and that trade is not optional. A big static (effR ~900 u,
    // flora_tree_04) must now be hidden across a ~900 u wide footprint at the halfway point rather
    // than along one 64 u line — so the large near objects, which are exactly the ones a false
    // positive is most visible on, stop being rejected on a single unlucky bearing. Small distant
    // instances are barely affected: their cone is a few units wide and they are most of the set.
    LOOP
    for (int k = 1; k <= steps; ++k)
    {
        float  t = tEnd * ((float)k / (float)steps);
        float3 p = S + (tgt - S) * t;
        // u = fraction along the FULL eyeM->target ray, which is what the cone radius scales with —
        // t alone is the fraction along the ABOVE-WATER segment and would under-estimate the cone
        // near the surface, i.e. err back toward over-rejecting.
        float  u = t0 + (1.0f - t0) * t;
        float  coneR = effR * u;

        // The level whose texel spans the cone's DIAMETER, floored by the caller's level (which is
        // itself never below 1 — level 0 is the raw max field and is not conservative).
        float  lvlF = ceil(log2(max(2.0f * coneR / texel, 1.0f)));
        float  lvl  = clamp(max(lvlF, (float)level), 1.0f, maxLevel);
        float  tw   = texel * exp2(lvl);
        float  lres = max(res / exp2(lvl), 1.0f);
        float  inv  = 1.0f / tw;

        int2 q0 = int2(floor((p.xy - coneR - mp.xy) * inv));
        int2 q1 = int2(floor((p.xy + coneR - mp.xy) * inv));
        // ⚠ ANY corner off the window disarms the whole step. Off the map we do not know what is
        // there, and a min that quietly skipped the part it could not see would be asserting
        // occlusion from missing data — the one direction this may never fail in.
        if (q0.x < 0 || q0.y < 0 || q1.x < 0 || q1.y < 0)
            continue;
        if ((float)q1.x >= lres || (float)q1.y >= lres || (float)q0.x >= lres || (float)q0.y >= lres)
            continue;

        // The pyramid's MIN over each corner texel. An empty footprint carries the hugely negative
        // SKY_HEIGHT_NONE sentinel, which LOSES this min with no branch — a gap in the footprint
        // cannot claim to block anything, which is the whole reason the field is a min.
        int ilvl = (int)lvl;
        float h =        LoadTex2D(gSkyHeightMin, NO_SAMPLER, int2(q0.x, q0.y), ilvl).r;
        h = min(h,       LoadTex2D(gSkyHeightMin, NO_SAMPLER, int2(q1.x, q0.y), ilvl).r);
        h = min(h,       LoadTex2D(gSkyHeightMin, NO_SAMPLER, int2(q0.x, q1.y), ilvl).r);
        h = min(h,       LoadTex2D(gSkyHeightMin, NO_SAMPLER, int2(q1.x, q1.y), ilvl).r);

        // ⚠ NO `+ coneR` HERE. The horizontal widening above is the whole fix; the top ray already
        // IS the top of the cone (see the target note), so adding the radius again would demand
        // twice the clearance geometry asks for. Measured: the double-counted form rejected 69.6%
        // against this one's larger figure, for no correctness gained.
        if (h > p.z + bias)
            return true;
    }
    return false;
}

// ─── DISTANT-STATICS LOD (Release 1: G7-matched geometry) ────────────────────────────────────────
// Rides the same cbuffer's spare lanes: arm2.z = k, arm2.w = the LOD block's subset OFFSET (the
// host appends one simplified copy of every statics subset after the originals, in the same order).
// An instance takes its simplified subsets when effR < k * distance, i.e. when its bound sphere
// subtends less than k radians — the simplifier's error budget is a fraction of the mesh size, so a
// fixed angular size holds the on-screen error under a pixel. k = 0 (the grass lane's buffer, and
// lodStatics off) = always the originals. cull.comp and cullscatter.comp MUST call this with the
// same arguments, or the count and the scatter disagree about which subset an instance fills.
uint cullLodOffset(float effR, float dist2)
{
    const float k = gHeightOccParams.arm2.z;
    if (!(k > 0.0f)) { return 0u; }
    return (effR * effR < k * k * dist2) ? (uint)gHeightOccParams.arm2.w : 0u;
}
